---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import TrialCta from '../components/TrialCta.astro';
---
<Base
  title="About Bright School | Live Coding and AI Classes for Kids"
  description="Bright School has taught children to code since 2020. Meet founder Gautam Jain and read why we think every child can be a creator, not only a user, of technology."
>
  <section class="wrap page-head">
    <p class="tag">Teaching since 2020</p>
    <h1>Every child can be a creator</h1>
    <p class="lead">Bright School teaches children aged 6 to 15 to code and to understand AI, in live classes with a real teacher.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2 style="margin-top: 0">Why Gautam started Bright School</h2>
      <p>Gautam Jain has loved computers and games since childhood. When he made his first game in 2014, he realised the power of coding, and that every kid can live the dream and be a creator.</p>
      <p>He believes children should be educated beyond school. Coding shapes thinking and curiosity from early childhood, and drives children to become the creators of the future.</p>

      <h2>Why coding, and why now</h2>
      <p>Children already spend hours with technology. Coding turns that time around: instead of only playing a game, a child plans one, builds it, finds out why it breaks and fixes it.</p>
      <p>That process teaches four things we care about more than any single programming language:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Logical thinking.</strong> Breaking a big idea into steps a computer can follow.</li>
        <li><strong>Problem-solving.</strong> Testing, finding the bug, and trying again without giving up.</li>
        <li><strong>Creativity.</strong> Every project is the child's own story, game or app.</li>
        <li><strong>Storytelling.</strong> Explaining what they made and why it works.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Why AI is part of it</h2>
      <p>Today's children will grow up with AI the way their parents grew up with the internet. We want them to know how it works, where it goes wrong, and how to use it safely, and not only how to type a question into it.</p>
      <p>So AI runs through the curriculum: standalone modules that need no coding, and a builders' track for students who know Python. Every AI tool is used with a teacher present in the class.</p>

      <h2>How we teach</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Live, with a teacher.</strong> Every class is 60 minutes, online, and never a recording.</li>
        <li><strong>Small.</strong> 1:1 or groups of up to 3, so every child is heard.</li>
        <li><strong>At the child's pace.</strong> The teacher moves on when the child is ready, not when the calendar says so.</li>
        <li><strong>Built around projects.</strong> Every module ends with something the child made themselves.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>See the full path in <a href="/curriculum">the curriculum</a>, or read the <a href="/faq">questions parents ask most</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <TrialCta />
</Base>
